B1|A Different Way to Remember|Doyun studied a list of twenty words by reading each word and its meaning ten times. He felt confident when the words looked familiar. The next day, however, he could not remember several meanings without seeing his notes. His friend suggested covering the meanings and trying to recall them before checking the answers. The method felt harder, but it showed exactly which words needed more practice. Doyun also reviewed these words again a few days later. He noticed that feeling familiar with an answer was not always the same as being able to produce it. A small test without hints can reveal the difference between recognition and memory.|친숙하게 느끼는 것과 스스로 기억해 내는 것은 다를 수 있다|여러 번 읽으면 누구나 모든 단어를 완전히 기억한다|힌트 없는 연습은 단어 학습에 전혀 도움이 되지 않는다|복습은 항상 같은 날 한꺼번에 해야 한다|A small test without hints can reveal the difference between recognition and memory.|도윤의 친구가 권한 방법은?|뜻을 가리고 먼저 떠올린 뒤 확인하기|노트를 보면서 의미를 계속 읽기|모르는 단어는 영구히 제외하기|친구의 단어장을 버리기|His friend suggested covering the meanings and trying to recall them before checking the answers.|learning
B2|A Window for Everyone|A community center planned to replace old windows to reduce heating costs. The manager chose a design that looked most efficient on paper. During a meeting, however, several visitors pointed out that the new windows were difficult to open for people with limited hand strength. The center compared other designs and found one that saved slightly less energy but was easier for more visitors to use. The staff did not ignore efficiency. They reconsidered how it should be measured alongside accessibility. A solution that performs well on one technical measure may still fail to serve the people for whom it was built.|기술적 효율성만으로 사용자를 위한 좋은 설계를 판단할 수 없다|가장 에너지 효율적인 설계는 언제나 모두 사용하기 쉽다|이용자의 신체적 조건은 창문 선택과 무관하다|접근성을 고려하면 에너지 절약이 완전히 불가능하다|A solution that performs well on one technical measure may still fail to serve the people for whom it was built.|회의에서 기존 설계의 문제로 지적된 것은?|손 힘이 약한 이용자는 창문을 열기 어려움|건물에 창문이 하나도 없음|창문이 모두 자주색이었음|난방 장치가 작동하지 않음|the new windows were difficult to open for people with limited hand strength.|design
B2|A Number Without a Story|An online report announced that a school's reading score had risen by fifteen points. The headline sounded impressive. A student asked whether the test had changed, how many pupils had taken it, and what the original score had been. The report did not provide those details. Her teacher explained that a number can describe a real change while still leaving important questions unanswered. The class searched for the full report and discovered that the school had introduced a different test. They could not directly compare the two scores without more information. Numerical evidence becomes meaningful only when readers understand how it was produced and what it can legitimately support.|수치 자료를 해석하려면 측정 방법과 비교 조건을 확인해야 한다|점수가 오르면 시험이 달라져도 반드시 완전히 같은 기준이다|기사 제목에 수치가 있으면 추가 정보는 불필요하다|원점수와 응시자 수는 자료 해석에 전혀 영향을 주지 않는다|Numerical evidence becomes meaningful only when readers understand how it was produced and what it can legitimately support.|수업에서 발견한 비교의 문제는?|학교가 서로 다른 시험을 사용했다는 점|점수가 모두 0이었다는 점|시험 시간이 항상 동일했다는 점|기록을 잃어버렸다는 점|the school had introduced a different test.|data
B3|The Efficient but Fragile System|A town connected all neighborhood stores to one central warehouse. On ordinary days, the arrangement lowered transport costs because trucks could carry fuller loads. The town council proudly reported the savings. Then a road closure prevented vehicles from reaching the warehouse for two days. Stores on that side of town quickly ran short of certain products, even though supplies existed elsewhere. The council realized that it had measured efficiency under typical conditions but not the consequences of a rare disruption. Members explored backup routes and smaller storage points. These options would increase costs in ordinary weeks, yet they might protect essential deliveries during emergencies. Evaluating a system requires attention to both daily performance and its ability to absorb unexpected failures.|시스템 평가는 평상시 효율성과 충격에 대한 대응 능력을 함께 고려해야 한다|운송비가 낮으면 공급 중단은 결코 발생하지 않는다|중앙창고는 언제나 여러 분산창고보다 안전하다|비상 대응책은 반드시 평상시 비용까지 줄인다|Evaluating a system requires attention to both daily performance and its ability to absorb unexpected failures.|도로 폐쇄로 즉시 영향을 받은 것은?|창고에 접근하지 못한 지역 상점의 재고|모든 상점의 건물 크기|마을의 전기 생산량|모든 운전자의 면허|Stores on that side of town quickly ran short of certain products, even though supplies existed elsewhere.|logistics
